219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 141 0 R
/T (This project presents a comparative performance analysis of Single-Cycle and 5-Stage Pipelined processor architectures using the Ripes simulator based on the RISC-V architecture. The main objective of this study is to understand how processor design influences execution speed, clock cycles, and overall efficiency. In a Single-Cycle processor, each instruction is executed in one long clock cycle, making the design simple but slower due to increased clock period. In contrast, a Pipelined processor divides instruction execution into five stages: Instruction Fetch \(IF\), Instruction Decode \(ID\), Execute \(EX\), Memory Access \(MEM\), and Write Back \(WB\). This allows multiple instructions to overlap in execution, improving throughput. Using the Ripes simulator, sample RISC-V assembly programs are executed on both processor models. Key performance metrics such as Clock Cycles, Cycles Per Instruction \(CPI\), and instruction throughput are observed and compared. The study also analyzes the impact of pipeline hazards and stalls on performance. The results demonstrate that while the Single-Cycle processor offers simplicity in design, the Pipelined processor achieves higher efficiency and better performance due to parallel instruction processing. This project provides practical insight into modern processor architecture and helps in understanding the importance of pipelining in improving computational performance. )
/E (This project presents a comparative performance analysis of Single-Cycle and 5-Stage Pipelined processor architectures using the Ripes simulator based on the RISC-V architecture. The main objective of this study is to understand how processor design influences execution speed, clock cycles, and overall efficiency. In a Single-Cycle processor, each instruction is executed in one long clock cycle, making the design simple but slower due to increased clock period. In contrast, a Pipelined processor divides instruction execution into five stages: Instruction Fetch \(IF\), Instruction Decode \(ID\), Execute \(EX\), Memory Access \(MEM\), and Write Back \(WB\). This allows multiple instructions to overlap in execution, improving throughput. Using the Ripes simulator, sample RISC-V assembly programs are executed on both processor models. Key performance metrics such as Clock Cycles, Cycles Per Instruction \(CPI\), and instruction throughput are observed and compared. The study also analyzes the impact of pipeline hazards and stalls on performance. The results demonstrate that while the Single-Cycle processor offers simplicity in design, the Pipelined processor achieves higher efficiency and better performance due to parallel instruction processing. This project provides practical insight into modern processor architecture and helps in understanding the importance of pipelining in improving computational performance. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
