>>
endobj
278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 144 0 R
/T (In modern computer systems, processor performance plays a crucial role in determining the overall efficiency of computing systems. The design of the processor significantly affects execution speed, power consumption, and throughput. Among various processor architectures, Single-Cycle and Pipelined processors are fundamental designs used to understand performance optimization. A Single-Cycle processor executes each instruction in a single clock cycle. Although this design is simple and easy to implement, it suffers from poor performance due to long clock cycle duration. Each instruction must complete all stages such as fetch, decode, execute, memory access, and write-back in one cycle, resulting in inefficient utilization of hardware resources. In contrast, a Pipelined processor divides instruction execution into multiple stages. The most common design is a 5-stage pipeline consisting of Instruction Fetch \(IF\), Instruction Decode \(ID\), Execute \(EX\), Memory Access \(MEM\), and Write Back \(WB\). This allows multiple instructions to be processed simultaneously, thereby improving throughput and efficiency. This project focuses on analyzing the performance differences between Single-Cycle and Pipelined RISC-V processors using the Ripes simulator. Key metrics such as clock cycles, CPI \(Cycles Per Instruction\), and execution time are evaluated. Additionally, pipeline hazards and their effects on performance are studied.)
/E (In modern computer systems, processor performance plays a crucial role in determining the overall efficiency of computing systems. The design of the processor significantly affects execution speed, power consumption, and throughput. Among various processor architectures, Single-Cycle and Pipelined processors are fundamental designs used to understand performance optimization. A Single-Cycle processor executes each instruction in a single clock cycle. Although this design is simple and easy to implement, it suffers from poor performance due to long clock cycle duration. Each instruction must complete all stages such as fetch, decode, execute, memory access, and write-back in one cycle, resulting in inefficient utilization of hardware resources. In contrast, a Pipelined processor divides instruction execution into multiple stages. The most common design is a 5-stage pipeline consisting of Instruction Fetch \(IF\), Instruction Decode \(ID\), Execute \(EX\), Memory Access \(MEM\), and Write Back \(WB\). This allows multiple instructions to be processed simultaneously, thereby improving throughput and efficiency. This project focuses on analyzing the performance differences between Single-Cycle and Pipelined RISC-V processors using the Ripes simulator. Key metrics such as clock cycles, CPI \(Cycles Per Instruction\), and execution time are evaluated. Additionally, pipeline hazards and their effects on performance are studied.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
